The command litar-web serves a browser interface for compositions.
It runs litar -a and reads everything through that MCP server,
so the browser sees the same resources and tools as an agent,
and the command line of litar stays as the design defines it.
@[cmd/litar-web/main.go@`go module@=
@<litar-web@:command@:Language@/Go@/File@/file@>@

The page is embedded beside the program, so the command is one file to install.
@[cmd/litar-web/index.html@`go module@=
@<Project@/litar@/Web@/page@>

@

@<litar-web@:command@:Language@/Go@/File@/package comment@=
// Command litar-web serves a browser interface for litar compositions.
@

@<litar-web@:command@:Language@/Go@/File@/import paths@+
"context"
_ "embed"
"encoding/json"
"io"
"net/http"
"os/exec"
"strings"

"github.com/modelcontextprotocol/go-sdk/mcp"
@

Embedded HTML page served as the root document of litar-web.
@<litar-web@:command@:Language@/Go@/File@/variables@+
//go:embed index.html
var indexHTML []byte

@? Project@/litar@/Go@/variables@/litar-web.indexHTML @

The page is served at the root, resources and tools below /api, all behind the guard.
@<litar-web@:command@:Language@/Go@/File@/main body@=
session, err := connect(command.flags.litar, command.flags.file)
if err != nil {
	fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, "litar-web:", err)
	os.Exit(1)
}
defer session.Close()
http.HandleFunc("GET /{$}", serveIndex)
http.HandleFunc("GET /api/resource", resourceHandler(session))
http.HandleFunc("GET /api/tools", toolsHandler(session))
http.HandleFunc("POST /api/tool", toolHandler(session))
fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, "litar-web: serving on http://%s/\n", command.flags.addr)
if err := http.ListenAndServe(command.flags.addr, guard(http.DefaultServeMux)); err != nil {
	fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, "litar-web:", err)
	os.Exit(1)
}
@~ Project@/litar@/Go@/functions@/litar-web.connect @~ Project@/litar@/Go@/functions@/litar-web.serveIndex @~ Project@/litar@/Go@/functions@/litar-web.resourceHandler @~ Project@/litar@/Go@/functions@/litar-web.toolsHandler @~ Project@/litar@/Go@/functions@/litar-web.toolHandler @~ Project@/litar@/Go@/functions@/litar-web.guard @
@<Chunk@/Project@/litar@/Web@=
{"description": "The page that litar-web serves: markup, style, and script."}
@
The litar tool: its Go module and build files.

The module path is litar, so the package reads litar/internal/litar in import paths.
@<Language@/Go@/Module@/project properties@+
module: 'litar',
go: '1.25.0',
@
The package litar holds the whole tool; the command litar only calls litar.Main.
Each file of the package carries its own label, its base name,
and a test file the base name of the file it tests followed by test.
@<litar@:Language@/Go@/File@/module properties@+
name: 'litar',
@
The command litar is a command of the Go file structure:
the flag package parses its invocation syntax, and the main body hands the flags to litar.Main.
